d, (4,1) allowed, (5,-2) not allowed. So line covers (3,4),(4,1). That's two points.\n\nSlope 3 lines: y = 3x + b. For x=3, y = 9 + b ≤ 4 => b ≤ -5. For x=4, y = 12 + b ≤ 3 => b ≤ -9. So b ≤ -9. For b=-9: (3,0) not allowed. So no points.\n\nThus any sunny line can have at most 2 points in columns 3-6 for n=6. So with 4 sunny lines, we can cover at most 8 points, but we need to cover 10 points. So k=4 impossible for n=6 as well.\n\nThus the solution's claim that k≥4 impossible seems plausible for all n≥3. Let's try to prove generally: For any n≥3, any sunny line can intersect at most 2 points of T_n? Actually earlier they argued that a sunny line can meet each column at most one point, but they didn't bound the total number of points a sunny line can contain. Could a sunny line contain more than 2 points? Let's examine.\n\nGiven a sunny line L with slope m = p/q (reduced), p,q integers, q>0, p≠0, p≠ -q. Points (x,y) ∈ Z^2 on L satisfy y = (p/q) x + b, where b rational. For integer points, we need x such that (p/q) x + b is integer. Since p/q is reduced, the set of integer points on L forms an arithmetic progression in x with step q (i.e., x ≡ x0 mod q). So the x-coordinates of integer points on L are of the form x = x0 + t q for integer t. So the difference in x between any two integer points is a multiple of q. So the number of integer points on L within the range of x from 1 to n is at most floor((n - x0)/q) + 1. So the maximum number of integer points on L in T_n is at most floor((n - something)/q) + 1. Since q≥1, the maximum is at most n (if q=1). But we also have the constraint that points must satisfy a+b ≤ n+1. So the sum s = x + y = x + (p/q) x + b = (p+q)/q x + b. So s is an arithmetic progression with step (p+q)/q = (p+q)/q. Since p+q ≠ 0 (p≠ -q), the step is non-zero. So the number of points with s ≤ n+1 is limited. The solution gave bound (3): floor((n-1)/|p+q|) + 1 ≤ floor(n/|p+q|) + 1. Since |p+q| ≥ 1, the bound is at most n+1. But more importantly, for small n, the bound may be small. For n≥3, the maximum number of points a sunny line can contain is at most floor((n-1)/|p+q|) + 1. For |p+q| = 1, the bound is floor((n-1)/1) + 1 = n. So a sunny line with p+q = ±1 could contain up to n points. But p+q = ±1 implies p = -q ± 1. Since p ≠ -q (excluded), p+q = 1 or -1 is allowed? Wait, p+q = 0 is excluded (p = -q). So p+q = ±1 is allowed. For p+q = 1, we have p = 1 - q. For q≥1, p can be negative or zero? p cannot be zero (non-horizontal). So p = 1 - q ≠ 0 => q ≠ 1. So q≥2, p = 1 - q ≤ -1. So slope p/q = (1 - q)/q = 1/q - 1. For q=2, p = -1, slope = -1/2. That's allowed (sunny). For q=3, p = -2, slope = -2/3. That's allowed. So lines with p+q = 1 have slope negative fraction less than -1? Actually slope = (1 - q)/q = 1/q - 1 = -(q-1)/q = -(1 - 1/q). So slope is between -1 and 0 (negative but > -1). For q=2, slope = -1/2; for q=3, slope = -2/3; for q=4, slope = -3/4; etc. So these slopes are allowed. For p+q = -1, p = -1 - q, slope = -(q+1)/q = -1 - 1/q, which is less than -1 (more negative). For q=1, p = -2, slope = -2 (allowed). So lines with p+q = -1 have slope less than -1.\n\nThus there exist sunny lines with |p+q| = 1, which can have up to n points (the bound says floor((n-1)/1)+1 = n). So a sunny line could potentially contain many points, maybe up to n. But does such a line actually intersect many points of T_n? Let's examine p+q = 1 case: slope = - (q-1)/q, intercept b such that integer points exist. For q=2, slope = -1/2, line equation: y = -x/2 + b. For integer points, x must be even. So x = 2t. Then y = -t + b. So points are (2t, -t + b). Condition a+b ≤ n+1 => 2t + (-t + b) = t + b ≤ n+1 => t ≤ n+1 - b. Also y > 0 => -t + b > 0 => t b. So t can range from 1 to min(b-1, n+1-b). So number of integer points is roughly min(b-1, n+1-b). This can be up to floor((n)/2) maybe. For n large, we can choose b appropriately to maximize number of points. For example, choose b = floor((n+2)/2). Then t can range from 1 to floor((n)/2). So number of points ~ floor(n/2). So a sunny line with slope -1/2 can contain about n/2 points. That's more than 2 for large n. So the earlier claim that a sunny line can have at most 2 points is false for larger n. However, the solution's argument for k≥4 uses only the fact that each sunny line can meet each column at most one point, not the total number of points. So they didn't rely on a bound on total points per line. So the argument that k≥4 impossible might be based on a counting of columns, not points. Let's examine more carefully.\n\nWe have n columns. Each sunny line can intersect each column at most once. So each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. So across all columns, a sunny line can cover at most n points (one per column). That's fine. So the capacity argument for covering points is not limited by per-line point count, but by per-column capacity: each column can have at most k points covered by sunny lines (since each sunny line can cover at most one point in that column). So for column i, we need to cover h_i points. If h_i > k, we need at least one non-sunny line covering the remaining points. Since a vertical line can cover all points in a column, we need at most one vertical line per column where h_i > k. So the number of columns with h_i > k must be ≤ number of non-sunny lines = n - k. Let's compute number of columns with h_i > k.\n\nRecall h_i = n+1 - i. So h_i > k => n+1 - i > k => i n+1 - k. So columns i = 1,2,..., n - k have h_i > k. That's exactly n - k columns. So we need at most n - k non-sunny lines, which we have exactly n - k. So the condition is satisfied exactly. So there is no immediate contradiction. So the solution's claim that k≥4 impossible is not justified by this counting. They claim \"Since a sunny line can meet each column in at most one point, having k≥4 would force at least four columns to rely on sunny lines for at least one of their points, leaving at most n-4 vertical lines to cover the remaining columns. Because the three rightmost columns already force the use of a vertical line for column n-2, we would run out of vertical lines before all columns are covered.\" This reasoning seems flawed: For k≥4, we have at most n-4 vertical lines, but we need vertical lines for columns where h_i > k. For k≥4, columns with h_i > k are those with i ≤ n - k. Since n - k ≤ n - 4, we need at most n - k vertical lines, which is ≤ n - 4. So we have enough vertical lines. The \"three rightmost columns already force the use of a vertical line for column n-2\" is only true for k ≤ 3, not for k≥4. So the argument is invalid.\n\nThus the solution's claim that k≥4 impossible is not justified. However, we need to check if there might be a more subtle reason that k≥4 is indeed impossible, perhaps due to geometry constraints beyond column counting. Let's explore more thoroughly.\n\nWe need to find a configuration for k=4 for some n≥3. Let's try n=7. T_7 has columns sizes: h_1=7, h_2=6, h_3=5, h_4=4, h_5=3, h_6=2, h_7=1. We need 7 lines total, with 4 sunny lines and 3 non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines can be vertical lines covering columns fully. We have 3 vertical lines, so we can cover 3 columns fully. Which columns should we cover? Likely the largest columns: columns 1,2,3 (sizes 7,6,5). Then remaining columns 4-7 have sizes 4,3,2,1. We have 4 sunny lines to cover those. Each column has at most 4 points, which is ≤ k=4, so each column can be covered by sunny lines. The question is whether we can assign points to 4 lines such that each line has at most one point per column and the points assigned to each line are collinear (sunny). This is a combinatorial geometry problem. For n=7, columns 4-7 have total points = 4+3+2+1 = 10 points. We have 4 lines, average 2.5 points per line. Could we find 4 sunny lines covering all 10 points? Possibly.\n\nWe need to see if there is a known result: The problem is reminiscent of covering a triangular grid with lines, with constraints on slopes. The solution claims only k=0,1,3 possible. But we suspect k=4 might be possible for larger n. Let's try to construct for n=7.\n\nWe need to cover points in columns 4-7:\n\nColumn 4 (x=4): y=1,2,3,4 (since 4+4=8 ≤ 8? Wait n+1=8, so y ≤ 8-4=4). So points: (4,1),(4,2),(4,3),(4,4).\n\nColumn 5 (x=5): y=1,2,3 (since 5+3=8). Points: (5,1),(5,2),(5,3).\n\nColumn 6 (x=6): y=1,2 (since 6+2=8). Points: (6,1),(6,2).\n\nColumn 7 (x=7): y=1 (since 7+1=8). Point: (7,1).\n\nTotal points = 4+3+2+1 = 10.\n\nWe need 4 sunny lines covering these points. Let's try to find lines that can cover multiple points.\n\nPotential slopes: integer slopes p (since q=1) can give many points. For slope p=1, line y = x + b. For x=4, y = 4+b ≤ 4 => b ≤ 0. For x=5, y = 5+b ≤ 3 => b ≤ -2. So b must be ≤ -2 to have point at column 5. Then at column 4, y = 4-2 = 2 (allowed). At column 6, y = 6-2 = 4 > 2 not allowed. So line y = x - 2 passes through (4,2) and (5,3) (since 5-2=3). Also (6,4) not allowed. So line L1: (4,2),(5,3). That's two points.\n\nSlope p=2: y = 2x + b. For x=4, y = 8+b ≤ 4 => b ≤ -4. For x=5, y = 10+b ≤ 3 => b ≤ -7. So b ≤ -7. Then at x=4, y = 8-7=1 (allowed). At x=5, y = 10-7=3 (allowed). At x=6, y = 12-7=5 > 2 not allowed. So line L2: (4,1),(5,3). That's two points.\n\nSlope p=-1: not allowed.\n\nSlope p=-2: y = -2x + b. For x=4, y = -8 + b ≤ 4 => b ≤ 12. For x=5, y = -10 + b ≤ 3 => b ≤ 13. So b can be up to 12. For b=12: (4,4),(5,2). Also (6,0) not allowed. So line L3: (4,4),(5,2). That's two points.\n\nSlope p=-3: y = -3x + b. For x=4, y = -12 + b ≤ 4 => b ≤ 16. For x=5, y = -15 + b ≤ 3 => b ≤ 18. So b up to 16. For b=16: (4,4),(5,1). So line L4: (4,4),(5,1). That's two points.\n\nSlope p=-1/2: y = -x/2 + b. For x even. For x=4, y = -2 + b ≤ 4 => b ≤ 6. For x=6, y = -3 + b ≤ 2 => b ≤ 5. So b ≤ 5. For b=5: (4,3),(6,2). Also (2,4) not in set. So line L5: (4,3),(6,2). That's two points.\n\nSlope p=-2/3: y = -2x/3 + b. For x multiple of 3. For x=6, y = -4 + b ≤ 2 => b ≤ 6. For x=3 not in set. For x=6, b=6 gives y=2 (allowed). For x=3, not needed. For x=6, b=5 gives y=1 (allowed). So line L6: (6,2) with b=6? Actually (6,2) => -2*6/3 + b = -4 + b = 2 => b=6. So line y = -2x/3 + 6 passes through (6,2). Does it pass through any other points in columns 4-7? For x=3, y = -2 + 6 = 4 (point (3,4) not in our set). For x=0, y=6 (not relevant). So only one point.\n\nSlope p=1/2: y = x/2 + b. For x even. For x=4, y = 2 + b ≤ 4 => b ≤ 2. For x=6, y = 3 + b ≤ 2 => b ≤ -1. So b ≤ -1. For b=-1: (4,1) (since 2-1=1), (6,2) (3-1=2). So line L7: (4,1),(6,2). That's two points.\n\nSlope p=3: y = 3x + b. For x=4, y = 12 + b ≤ 4 => b ≤ -8. For x=5, y = 15 + b ≤ 3 => b ≤ -12. So b ≤ -12. For b=-12: (4,0) not allowed. So no points.\n\nThus many lines can cover at most 2 points. However, there might be lines that cover 3 points across columns 4-7. Let's search for lines with slope -1/3: y = -x/3 + b. For x=6, y = -2 + b ≤ 2 => b ≤ 4. For x=3, not in set. For x=6, b=4 gives y=2 (point (6,2)). For x=3, y = -1 + 4 = 3 (point (3,3) not in set). For x=9, not relevant. So only one point.\n\nSlope -2: we already considered.\n\nSlope -1/2 lines can have points at x=4,6, maybe also x=2 (not in set). So at most 2 points.\n\nThus it seems each sunny line can cover at most 2 points in columns 4-7 for n=7. But is that always true? Let's consider slope -1/3 lines with x=5? x=5 not multiple of 3, so not integer y.\n\nThus for columns 4-7, each sunny line can cover at most 2 points. So with 4 sunny lines, we can cover at most 8 points, but we need 10 points. So k=4 impossible for n=7 as well.\n\nBut maybe for larger n, we can have sunny lines covering more points across columns. Let's examine general pattern: For columns near the rightmost side, the column sizes are small (3,2,1). So the maximum number of points per column is small. The difficulty is covering all points with limited sunny lines. The solution's argument for k≥4 uses the fact that the three rightmost columns already force a vertical line for column n-2 when k ≤ 3. But for k≥4, we might be able to cover those columns with sunny lines. However, the total number of points in the rightmost columns is 1+2+3 = 6. With k=4, we have 4 sunny lines, each can cover at most one point per column, so across the three columns, we can cover at most 4*3 = 12 points, which is enough for 6 points. So no issue.\n\nBut the problem is the leftmost columns have many points. For n large, the leftmost columns have many points, but we can use vertical lines to cover them. The number of vertical lines we have is n - k. For k=4, we have n-4 vertical lines. We need to cover columns where h_i > k = 4. That is columns i where n+1 - i > 4 => i n - 3. So columns 1 to n-4 have h_i > 4. That's n-4 columns. So we need vertical lines for each of those columns. Since we have exactly n-4 vertical lines, we can assign one vertical line per column for columns 1 to n-4. Then the remaining columns are i = n-3, n-2, n-1, n. Those have h_i = 4,3,2,1 respectively. So we need to cover these columns with sunny lines. Since we have 4 sunny lines, each column can have at most 4 points covered by sunny lines, which is enough. So the capacity is fine.\n\nThus the existence of a configuration reduces to the existence of 4 sunny lines covering all points in the last 4 columns (i.e., columns n-3 to n). Let's denote these columns as C_{n-3}, C_{n-2}, C_{n-1}, C_n. Their sizes are 4,3,2,1 respectively. So total points = 10. We need to cover them with 4 sunny lines. Each line can intersect each column at most once, so each line can cover at most 4 points (one per column). So we need to partition the 10 points into 4 groups, each group having at most one point per column, and each group must be collinear (sunny). This is a combinatorial geometry problem.\n\nThe solution for k=3 gave a construction covering the last 3 columns (sizes 3,2,1) with 3 sunny lines, each covering exactly one point per column (so each line covers 3 points). For k=4, we need to cover the last 4 columns (sizes 4,3,2,1) with 4 sunny lines. Is that possible? Let's try to find a construction for general n.\n\nWe can try to generalize the construction for k=3: they used three lines each covering two points in column n-2, one point in column n-1, one point in column n. For k=4, we need to cover column n-3 (size 4) as well. Perhaps we can add a fourth sunny line that covers the remaining point in column n-3 and also covers points in other columns. Let's attempt to construct for n=7 (so columns 4-7). The solution for k=3 used lines:\n\nL1: through (n,1) and (n-2,2) => (7,1) and (5,2)\nL2: through (n-1,1) and (n-2,3) => (6,1) and (5,3)\nL3: through (n-2,1) and (n-1,2) => (5,1) and (6,2)\n\nThese three lines cover points: (7,1),(6,1),(5,1),(5,2),(5,3),(6,2). That's 6 points, covering columns 5,6,7. For n=7, column 5 has points (5,1),(5,2),(5,3). Column 6 has (6,1),(6,2). Column 7 has (7,1). So they covered all points in columns 5-7. For k=4, we need to also cover column 4 (points (4,1),(4,2),(4,3),(4,4)). We have an extra sunny line to cover these 4 points. Can we find a sunny line that passes through one point in each of columns 4,5,6,7? That would be ideal: a line that intersects each of the four columns at one point, covering 4 points. Then the other three sunny lines can cover the remaining points in columns 5-7 as before. Let's see if such a line exists.\n\nWe need a line L4 that passes through (4, y4), (5, y5), (6, y6), (7, y7) with y4 ≤ 4, y5 ≤ 3, y6 ≤ 2, y7 ≤ 1, and all y_i positive integers. Also the line must be sunny: slope not 0, ∞, -1.\n\nLet’s denote points: (4, a), (5, b), (6, c), (7, d). Since the line is straight, the differences must be consistent: slope = (b - a) / (5 - 4) = b - a. Similarly, c - b = slope, d - c = slope. So we need a, b, c, d forming an arithmetic progression with common difference s = b - a = c - b = d - c. So a, a+s, a+2s, a+3s must be integers satisfying constraints:\n\n- a ∈ {1,2,3,4}\n- a+s ∈ {1,2,3} (since b ≤ 3)\n- a+2s ∈ {1,2} (since c ≤ 2)\n- a+3s ∈ {1} (since d ≤ 1)\n\nAlso all values must be ≥1.\n\nThus a+3s = 1 => s = (1 - a)/3. Since a is integer 1..4, compute possibilities:\n\n- a=1 => s = 0 => slope 0 (horizontal) not allowed.\n- a=2 => s = (1-2)/3 = -1/3 => not integer, but slope can be rational, but we need integer points. However, if s = -1/3, then b = a + s = 2 - 1/3 = 5/3 not integer, so not allowed.\n- a=3 => s = (1-3)/3 = -2/3 => b = 3 - 2/3 = 7/3 not integer.\n- a=4 => s = (1-4)/3 = -1 => s = -1 => slope -1 (not allowed). Also b = 4 - 1 = 3 (allowed), c = 2 (allowed), d = 1 (allowed). So points would be (4,4),(5,3),(6,2),(7,1). This line has slope -1, which is not sunny. So not allowed.\n\nThus there is no sunny line covering one point in each of columns 4-7. So we cannot cover column 4 with a single line that also covers points in columns 5-7. So we need to use multiple lines to cover column 4 points. Since we have only one extra sunny line (the fourth), we need to allocate column 4 points across the four sunny lines. The three lines used for columns 5-7 already cover some points in column 5 (three points), column 6 (two points), column 7 (one point). They do not cover any points in column 4. So we need to cover the four points in column 4 using the fourth sunny line. But a single line can intersect column 4 at at most one point. So we cannot cover all four points in column 4 with a single sunny line. Therefore k=4 impossible for n=7.\n\nThus the solution's claim that k≥4 impossible seems plausible: because the three rightmost columns have sizes 3,2,1, and we need to cover the column n-3 (size 4) as well. With k=4, we have only 4 sunny lines, but we need to cover column n-3 (size 4) with at most one point per line, so we need each sunny line to intersect column n-3 at a distinct point. That uses up all 4 sunny lines for column n-3. Then we have no sunny lines left to cover columns n-2, n-1, n. However, we could use vertical lines for those columns? But those columns have small sizes, but we have no vertical lines left because we used all n - k vertical lines for columns 1..n-4. Wait, for k=4, we have n - 4 vertical lines. We used them for columns 1..n-4. So we have no vertical lines left for columns n-3..n. So we must cover columns n-3..n entirely with sunny lines. Since we have exactly 4 sunny lines, each can intersect each column at most once, so each column can have at most 4 points covered by sunny lines. Column n-3 has 4 points, so each sunny line must intersect column n-3 at exactly one point. That uses up all 4 sunny lines for column n-3. Then for column n-2 (size 3), we need to cover its 3 points with sunny lines, but each sunny line already used its intersection with column n-3; can a line intersect two columns? Yes, a line can intersect multiple columns, but each line can intersect each column at most once. So a line that already intersected column n-3 can also intersect column n-2 at a point (different column). So we can still use the same 4 lines to cover points in column n-2 as well. However, each line can intersect column n-2 at at most one point, so across 4 lines we can cover up to 4 points in column n-2, which is enough for its 3 points. So that's okay. Similarly for column n-1 (size 2) and column n (size 1). So the capacity is fine. The issue is whether we can assign points to lines such that each line is collinear across columns. That is the main difficulty.\n\nThe solution for k=3 succeeded because they could find 3 lines that each intersect columns n-2, n-1, n at appropriate points. For k=4, we need 4 lines that intersect columns n-3, n-2, n-1, n. The question is whether such a set of 4 lines exists. The earlier attempt to find a line intersecting all four columns gave slope -1 (not allowed). So maybe we need to use a different arrangement: not all lines need to intersect all four columns; some lines may intersect only a subset. But we need to cover all points in column n-3 (four