 to cover points:\n\nColumn 4: A1=(4,1), A2=(4,2), A3=(4,3), A4=(4,4)\nColumn 5: B1=(5,1), B2=(5,2), B3=(5,3)\nColumn 6: C1=(6,1), C2=(6,2)\nColumn 7: D1=(7,1)\n\nWe have 4 lines L1..L4.\n\nWe need to assign each point to a line such that each line has at most one point per column, and points on each line are collinear with a sunny slope.\n\nWe can try to use the three lines from k=3 solution for columns 5-7: L1: (7,1)-(5,2) slope? Let's compute slope: (1-2)/(7-5) = -1/2, sunny. L2: (6,1)-(5,3) slope = (1-3)/(6-5) = -2, sunny. L3: (5,1)-(6,2) slope = (1-2)/(5-6) = 1, sunny. These three lines cover points: D1, B2; C1, B3; B1, C2. So they cover B1, B2, B3, C1, C2, D1. That's all points in columns 5-7. So after using these three lines, we have covered all points in columns 5-7. The remaining points are column 4: A1..A4. We have one remaining sunny line L4 to cover all four points in column 4. But a line can intersect column 4 at only one point, so cannot cover all four. So we need to adjust the assignment: maybe we can reassign some points from columns 5-7 to L4, so that L4 also covers some points in those columns, allowing L1-L3 to cover some points in column 4. That way we can distribute the column 4 points across the four lines.\n\nThus we need to find a set of four lines such that each line covers one point in column 4, and collectively they cover all points in columns 5-7 as well. Let's try to find lines that pass through a point in column 4 and also a point in column 5,6,7.\n\nWe can try to find a line through (4,4) and (7,1). Slope = (1-4)/(7-4) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed. So (4,4) cannot be paired with (7,1) on a sunny line.\n\nTry (4,3) and (7,1): slope = (1-3)/(7-4) = -2/3, allowed (sunny). So line L_a: passes through (4,3) and (7,1). Does it also pass through any other points? Let's compute equation: slope m = -2/3. Using point (4,3): y - 3 = -2/3 (x - 4). So y = -2/3 x + (8/3) + 3 = -2/3 x + 8/3 + 9/3 = -2/3 x + 17/3. For integer x values, we need y integer. 